it was 5 55 in the morning and much of  the nipro was asleep  only to be woken to this  one of three explosions that rocked the  city  caught on surveillance camera  oksana didn't hear the air raid sirens  but the force of the blast shattered the  windows and her ceiling collapsed  she lives on the fourth floor of an  apartment block facing a shoe factory an  apparent target of russian missiles  we didn't expect anything in nepal there  are no military facilities around the  russians were saying that they would  only target military infrastructure here  there are only residential blocks the  school and the kindergarten windows were  hit  the ukrainian military says the rockets  were launched from the donetsk region in  territory controlled by the  russian-backed separatists this is the  first time this city has been targeted  since the beginning of the war now that  will have a huge impact because so far  nepal has been a transit point for  people fleeing other areas in eastern  ukraine that were under heavy  bombardment  the immediate impact long queues outside  the train station  people standing for hours in sub-zero  temperature  patiently waiting to escape despite the  fear of another attack  katja took the decision to pack up and  leave as soon as she heard the early  morning explosions  it's a nightmare nato's not doing enough  they should close the sky they should do  more  many of those standing here had fled the  horrors of war once before their home  times already destroyed now seeking safe  refuge again  irina and her two children drove out of  kharkiv after russian jet fighters flew  over her neighborhood for days and the  shelling became closer and louder  this conflict has thrown her family  apart  zolensky won't do what russia expects  from him so we don't know what awaits us  of course ukraine will not compromise  my son also says no we were living in  hell in kharkiv for a week and now we  hate russia although we have many  relatives there  many of 